The libretro frontend as the arcade bar (web/emulator/libretro.js) drives it.

- Core options: the bar answers GET_VARIABLE only for the keys it sets; for every other key the
  fork used its C variables' initial values, which disagree with its own option table (empty
  joystick deadzone strings, no softlist/media type, so a game was started as a system "roms").
  Unanswered options now take the table's defaults, and the defaults are the bar's: thread mode
  off, lightgun mode "lightgun", mouse off, per-game button profiles on (Tekken 3's punches on
  Y X, kicks on B A, like its panel and the bar's keys A S / Z X); throttle, boot to OSD/BIOS,
  config read/write, auto save and cheats off, as before.
- Input descriptors: the loaded game's own, from MAME's input fields (named fields by their
  names, Tekken 3's unnamed four as Left/Right Punch/Kick), RetroPad and, for a lightgun game,
  lightgun; ordered stick, buttons, Start, Coin. Only fields the game reads as it is set up (a
  field's condition holds). For a driving game (retro_driving, which a game's button profile
  sets: patches/0007) the d-pad is the wheel ("Steer left"/"Steer right") and the pedals
  ("Accelerate" up, "Brake" down). The fork's generic "A"/"B"/... list goes.
- RETRO_MEMORY_SYSTEM_RAM: the main CPU's work RAM (a RAM device under it, else the largest RAM
  in its program map: System 23's 16 MB at 0). The fork's heuristic returned the base of whatever
  memory bank came first.
- mame_drc (default disabled): MAME's -drc / -nodrc, for CPUs with a recompiler (System 23's
  R4650). WebAssembly has only the recompiler's C backend, which in Chrome is slower than the
  interpreter (mame/README.md has the numbers). Both boards of a link use the same setting.
- MAME's -artwork_crop: the frame is the game's screen alone, not what a game's layout draws
  around it (Cruis'n USA's dashboard of button lamps made its frame 512x451, not 512x400).
- Pixels: RGBA8888 (the bar's format 100, bytes R G B A, opaque) when the frontend takes it,
  XRGB8888 otherwise. GET_AUDIO_VIDEO_ENABLE without video: the frame isn't rendered or sent.
- Determinism for rollback netplay: every retro_run runs exactly one frame (MAME's start-up text
  updated the video while loading, so the first call ran nothing and a machine that loaded a
  state before its first frame ran a frame behind); the machine's first frame runs inside
  retro_load_game (until then, what reset left for the next time slice, such as a sub-CPU or
  DSP held in reset, sits in anonymous timers that states leave out); inputs are read at the
  start of every frame from the first (the fork waited 5 frames, for the hiscore plugin); retro_reset happens at the
  start of the next frame and a state loaded before then cancels it (it used to happen a frame
  later, after the state); no RetroPad combination opens MAME's menu (Select + Button 3) or quits
  (Select + Start).
- A failed load returns false without the 1 s busy-wait, and later loads in that instance return
  false too (a second load after a failure crashed). serialize_size is 0 until a game is loaded.
- mame_state_layout(): offset, size and name of every saved item, for diagnosing states (not
  exported by default).

